  IBM Plex Sans JP as React Native needs it: one family the Android font stack can pick a weight from.

  The four static faces come from google/fonts (OFL 1.1, bundled in
  assets/licenses/IBMPlexSansJP-OFL.txt). One family covers the whole app: the JP faces carry the same
  IBM Plex Latin design as IBM Plex Sans (about 5 % larger on the em, to sit with the kana) alongside
  the Japanese glyphs, so a mixed string — "12 weeks · Anki", a gloss beside a headword — is drawn in
  one face with no per-script split. A glyph the face does not carry still falls back to the
  platform's own, as any font does.

  React Native hands a text's weight to `Typeface.create`, which resolves it against a family's
  declared entries, so every weight the type scale asks for has its own entry. Consumed through
  ReactFontManager.addCustomFont(..., R.font.ibm_plex_sans_jp) as the face "IBM Plex Sans JP"; the
  Compose overlay names the same four resources directly.
-->
<font-family xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <font
        android:font="@font/ibm_plex_sans_jp_regular"
        android:fontStyle="normal"
        android:fontWeight="400" />
    <font
        android:font="@font/ibm_plex_sans_jp_medium"
        android:fontStyle="normal"
        android:fontWeight="500" />
    <font
        android:font="@font/ibm_plex_sans_jp_semibold"
        android:fontStyle="normal"
        android:fontWeight="600" />
    <font
        android:font="@font/ibm_plex_sans_jp_bold"
        android:fontStyle="normal"
        android:fontWeight="700" />
</font-family>
